{
  "concept": "A pendulum's weight resolves into a component along the string (balanced by tension) and a tangential component −mg sin θ that always points back toward equilibrium, acting as the restoring force that drives oscillation.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Beige horizontal ceiling bar across the top of the figure",
    "Pivot point where the string attaches to the ceiling",
    "Dashed vertical line from pivot downward marking the equilibrium direction",
    "Solid string line from pivot to bob, labeled L",
    "Angle arc and label θ between the dashed vertical and the string, near the pivot",
    "Gray shaded sphere (bob) at the end of the string, labeled m",
    "Dashed arc from the bottom of the equilibrium line to the bob, labeled s (arc length displacement)",
    "Dark blue arrow along the string from the bob toward the pivot, labeled F_T (tension)",
    "Red arrow straight down from the bob, labeled w = mg",
    "Red arrow from bob along the string direction away from the pivot, labeled −mg cos θ",
    "Red arrow from bob tangent to the arc, pointing back toward equilibrium, labeled −mg sin θ",
    "Small angle arc labeled θ between the weight arrow and the −mg cos θ arrow",
    "Local axis pair at upper right: +y arrow along the string toward the pivot, +x arrow tangent to the arc away from equilibrium",
    "Dashed construction lines (parallelogram) showing the weight decomposed into its two components",
    "Optional overlay curve/label comparing sin θ to θ for the small-angle toggle",
    "Legend keying blue = tension, red = gravity and its components"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That as displacement grows, the tangential component −mg sin θ grows and always points back toward equilibrium, while the radial component −mg cos θ shrinks — and both vanish/maximize at predictable angles.",
      "label": "Angular displacement θ (degrees)",
      "range": [-60, 60, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "swing",
      "type": "animation",
      "teaches": "That the restoring force flips direction each time the bob crosses equilibrium, so the bob is always pulled back toward the center — the signature of oscillatory motion.",
      "label": "Release the pendulum",
      "loop": true
    },
    {
      "id": "forceModel",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That replacing sin θ with θ (small-angle approximation) makes the restoring force linear in displacement — the condition for simple harmonic motion — and that the approximation fails visibly at large angles.",
      "label": "Restoring force model",
      "options": ["Exact: −mg sin θ", "Small-angle: −mg θ", "Compare both"],
      "default": "Exact: −mg sin θ"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The pendulum at rest",
      "narration": "At θ = 0 the bob hangs on the dashed vertical. The weight w = mg points straight down along the string and is entirely balanced by the tension F_T — there is no tangential force, so nothing accelerates the bob.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 0, "forceModel": "Exact: −mg sin θ", "swing": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Displace the bob and split the weight",
      "narration": "Drag θ out to 25°. The weight now splits into −mg cos θ along the string (opposed by tension) and −mg sin θ tangent to the arc. Only the tangential piece is unbalanced, and it points back toward equilibrium along −x.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 25, "forceModel": "Exact: −mg sin θ", "swing": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Push to a large angle",
      "narration": "At 60° the tangential arrow is long and the radial arrow has shrunk. Notice the restoring force grows with displacement — but not in proportion to it, because it follows sin θ, not θ.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 60, "forceModel": "Exact: −mg sin θ", "swing": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sign flip through equilibrium",
      "narration": "Release the bob and watch. Each time it crosses the dashed vertical the tangential arrow reverses direction, so the force always points back toward the center — this is what sustains the oscillation.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 25, "forceModel": "Exact: −mg sin θ", "swing": "running" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The small-angle approximation",
      "narration": "Switch to the compare view. Below about 15° the −mg θ arrow overlaps the −mg sin θ arrow almost exactly: the restoring force is proportional to displacement, so the pendulum is a simple harmonic oscillator with period T = 2π√(L/g).",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 12, "forceModel": "Compare both", "swing": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Where the approximation breaks",
      "narration": "Push θ back out to 50° with both models drawn. The two arrows now visibly disagree — the true restoring force is weaker than the linear estimate, the motion is no longer simple harmonic, and the period lengthens.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 50, "forceModel": "Compare both", "swing": "idle" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Tension cancels only the radial part of gravity; the leftover tangential part, −mg sin θ, always points back toward the hanging position — and when θ is small enough that sin θ ≈ θ, that restoring force becomes proportional to displacement, which is exactly the definition of simple harmonic motion.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Portrait-oriented SVG diagram. A beige horizontal band spans the top as the ceiling. From a pivot near the top-center, a black dashed vertical line drops to mark equilibrium, and a solid black line runs down-and-right to the bob, labeled L near its midpoint. A small arc with label θ sits between the dashed vertical and the string at the pivot. The bob is a small radially-shaded gray sphere labeled m to its right. A black dashed arc labeled s sweeps from the foot of the dashed vertical to the bob. A dark navy-blue arrow runs from the bob back up along the string, labeled F_T. Three red/crimson arrows emanate from the bob: straight down labeled w = mg; along the string extended away from the pivot labeled −mg cos θ; and tangent to the arc pointing back toward equilibrium (down-and-left) labeled −mg sin θ. A small arc labeled θ sits between the vertical weight arrow and the −mg cos θ arrow. Upper right shows a thin black axis pair: +y arrow parallel to the string pointing toward the pivot, +x arrow perpendicular to it pointing away from equilibrium. Arrow lengths must scale live with θ: the tangential arrow ∝ sin θ, the radial arrow ∝ cos θ, weight arrow constant. In the compare mode draw the small-angle arrow in a lighter orange overlaid on the exact crimson arrow with a bracket showing the discrepancy, plus a small readout of sin θ vs θ in radians. Negative θ mirrors the whole construction to the left of the dashed vertical. Color key: navy = tension, crimson = gravity and components, orange = linear approximation, gray/black = geometry."
}

